<Base title="About Bradd McBrearty — Creative & Technical Art Director" description="A creative and technical art director, game design professor, and hands-on maker bringing 15+ years of production judgment to generative AI.">
<div class="wrap"><header class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Bradd McBrearty / Creative & Technical Art Director</p><h1>A maker’s eye.<br/><em>A director’s judgment.</em></h1></header><div class="about-grid"><figure class="about-portrait"><img src={url('/media/brand/bradd-headshot.webp')} alt="Bradd McBrearty" width="900" height="1125" fetchpriority="high"/></figure><div class="about-copy"><h2>I know the work<br/>from the inside.</h2><p>I’m a Creative Director and Technical Art Director for games, based in Oceanside, California. My career began in hands-on technical art and grew into directing the people, pipelines, and standards that bring a visual idea to life.</p><p>I’ve worked across real-time effects, shaders, characters, animation, environments, UI, and game systems. On Tetris Beat I built production tools and reactive visuals. On Ultimate Rivals I held the quality bar across disciplines. On Disney Enchanted Tales I critiqued the work of ten animators every day.</p><h2>The same bar.<br/>A different set of tools.</h2><p>Since 2022, I’ve applied that experience to generative AI: setting direction, reviewing output, and writing the rubrics that make critique useful. I decide what the work should do, where it falls short, and what should ship.</p><p>At Amber, I directed in-house models trained on wholly owned, rights-cleared assets and developed an ethical AI framework deployed across the studios. My approach keeps artists in charge of selection, correction, and finish.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/judging-ai-output/')}>Read how I judge AI output ↗</a></div></div><section class="teaching-band"><div><p class="eyebrow">Practice becomes a teachable standard</p><h2>Make the judgment<br/><em>useful to someone else.</em></h2></div><div><p>I teach game design and level design in the MFA in Game Design at Laguna College of Art and Design. I designed a 45-hour course that helps working professionals analyze Mechanics, Dynamics, and Aesthetics and use modern design frameworks.</p><p>Earlier, at The Art Institute of California, San Diego, I developed a Maya curriculum with more than 20 hours of video tutorials. Teaching and art direction ask for the same thing: see clearly, explain precisely, and help someone make better decisions.</p></div></section><p class="credits">Career and teaching details reflect my professional account. Project pages link to public artifacts and distinguish the parts I made, directed, and contributed to.</p></div>
</Base>
